  "query_preview": "..\nStrong winds and dry conditions will bring widespread critical to \nextreme fire weather conditions on Saturday...\n\nToday: A robust cold front moving through the region combined with \nvery dry conditions will result in extreme fire weather conditions \nacross the Inland Northwest. A strong upper level jet with 110+ kt \nflow is moving across the state of Washington as a deep low moves \nthrough British Columbia. This low will usher in a strong cold \nfront. Cold air advection and steep low level lapse rates will allow \n700mb 30-40kt winds to mix down to the surface. The NBM shows the \nentire forecast area with an 80% chance and above for maximum wind \ngusts above 30 mph. When looking at chances for 40+ mph, Spokane, \nPullman/Lewiston, the Columbia Basin, Waterville Plateau, and \nOkanogan Valley have a 30% chance and above. The Waterville Plateau \nhas the highest chances of seeing 50+ mph gusts, with a 70% chance \nand above. Minimum relative humidity values are expected to be \nbetween 15-25 percent. A Red Flag Warning has been issued regionwide \nthrough late this evening. A Particularly Dangerous Situation (PDS) \nRed Flag Warning has been issued for Colville, Okanogan Valley, \nWaterville Plateau, Western and Eastern Columbia Basin, Palouse, \nSnake River, and Spokane area. Relative humidity values are 10 to 15 \npercent in these areas. The PDS is valid from 2pm through 8pm. Main \nimpacts will be rapid fire spread and extreme fire behavior for any \nnew or ongoing fires. Please have multiple ways to receive \ninstructions from emergency officials. Expect wind impacts\nalongside fire weather impacts, such as trouble driving,\nespecially for high profile vehicles, minor tree damage, blowing\nobjects, and patchy blowing dust. \n\nSunday: Temperatures will be much cooler tomorrow in the wake of the \nfrontal passage, with highs in the mid to high 70s. This is around \n10 degrees cooler than is normal for this time of year. With the low \npressure still creating an 8-10mb gradient between SEA and MSO, \nwinds will continue to be breezy, but not near the level that they \nare today. Wind gusts along the Cascade gaps will be 25-30 mph \nSunday afternoon and evening. Throughout the Spokane, Coeur d'Alene, \nPullman, and Basin areas, wind gusts will be around 15-20 mph. Due \nto extreme fire weather conditions today, rapid fire spread is still \na possible impact for any ongoing fires tomorrow, even with higher \nrelative humidity values. \n\nMonday through Friday: Once the low moves out of the area, a ridge \nright along the PNW coast will strengthen. Nearly 100 percent of \nclusters support ridging across the western US throughout the next \nweek. This will result in temperatures warming back into the 90s by \nWednesday and isolated pockets of Moderate HeatRisk on Thursday.\nWinds along the Cascade gaps will be seasonably breezy, with gusts \n15-20 mph expected each afternoon and evening. There is a near zero \nchance for any precipitation within the next week for all areas \nexcept the Cascade gaps, so expect little to no relief from dry \nconditions. /AS",
